During my studies at university, I never took a course in differential geometry.
This is one of my biggest regrets as a student, since it seems like a very expressive and elegant theory, and it was easier back then to make time for such subjects.
I have since picked up copies of Lee's introductory texts [](cite:lee2012) and [](cite:lee2018), and I have been reading them routinely.
There is a lot of interplay between the primitive objects which define manifolds, and it is easy for me to lose sight of the big picture--especially if I find myself putting the book down for a few days (or \*gulp\* weeks).
In order to minimize this warmup cost, I have made a quick reference of these primitive objects and their basic properties.
I purposefully do not include deep technical details--that's what reading Lee is for.

# Manifolds

Before we get at all into the smooth or geometric structures, we have the notion of a _topological manifold_, which just gives us the _locally Euclidean_ notion and pins a fixed dimension for the space.

<Statement {...topoManifold} />

<Statement {...topoManifoldFigure} />

As the adjective suggests, topological manifolds are purely a topological concept, and their structure is sufficient for studying topological properties such as connectivity, compactness, and homeomorphic equivalence.
To discuss notions of calculus and geometry, we need additional structure: homeomorphisms do *not* preserve Euclidean differentiability, so there *must* be additional structure our manifolds hold.
Surely, whatever that structure is, it should imply our usual notion of smoothness in the coordinate representations  $\map \circ \chart^{-1}: \hat\open \rightarrow \bbR$.
In order for this to be the _defining_ property of smoothness of a map $\map: \manifold \rightarrow \bbR$, we need a structure on the coordinate charts that makes it well-defined.

<Statement {...smoothStructure} />

<Statement {...transitionFigure} />
